<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Changing Forests – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Changing Forests</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> How Our Ecology Is Changing</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Give a speech on an academic topic. Interactive: Engage in negotiations on various topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can give a speech about deforestation and its impact on local ecology (e.g., "Deforestation has affected biodiversity in our area.")<br>Can engage in role-play discussions about ecological changes in the local area and propose environmentally responsible actions (e.g., "If people planted more trees, the forests would recover faster.")<br>Can participate in negotiations about environmental changes and ask follow-up questions to encourage detailed answers (e.g., "If we limited urban expansion, would local wildlife have more space to thrive?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b><br>Students are engaged, the teacher models language, and understanding is clarified.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Display two contrasting images: one of a lush, healthy forest and another of a heavily deforested area. Ask students: "What differences do you observe between these two forest scenes? How has human activity impacted these ecosystems?" Encourage students to use vocabulary like 'deforestation', 'biodiversity', 'forest health'. (3 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences demonstrating the target grammar and vocabulary. "Deforestation has significantly affected biodiversity in our area, leading to a dramatic loss of species." "If we preserve our forests, we will help reduce illegal logging and improve air quality." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of the long vowel sound /aɪ/ in words like 'life', 'biodiversity', and 'try'. Also, demonstrate word stress in 'dramatic' and 'ecological'. (4 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'Deforestation has affected biodiversity', am I talking about an event in the distant past that is now finished, or an ongoing situation with current results?" (Expected answer: An ongoing situation with current results.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'biodiversity' mean in simple terms? Can you give an example of high biodiversity?" (Expected answer: Many different types of plants and animals in an area.)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher also briefly reviews the pronunciation of -ate endings, e.g., 'climate' /ɪt/. (3 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Introduction of language/content connected to the scenario using authentic input.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Changing Forests".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students listen to a short audio clip (1-2 minutes) of a local news report or a community activist discussing ecological changes in a Panamanian forest area, focusing on the impact of logging and urban expansion. The report will incorporate the target vocabulary and grammar structures (present perfect, conditionals, comparatives).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio news report/interview segment, informal but informative register, spoken word format.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Expressing environmental concerns politely. Pragmatic: Asking about environmental impact.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (oral, whole class):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main problem discussed in the report?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (oral check):</b> "True or False: The report states that urban ecosystems have increased biodiversity compared to natural habitats." (False, focuses on 'less biodiversity compared to rural or natural habitats'). "True or False: The activist believes planting trees can improve air quality." (True)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Activities focusing on accuracy and guided use of target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present perfect, first and second conditional, comparatives.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> deforestation, biodiversity, pollution, rivers, marine life, ecosystems, plastic waste, trees, air quality, climate change, forest, conservation, landfills, recycling, forest health, logging, species, explore, change, impact, reduce, plant, improve, fight, protect, recycle, accumulate, affect, contribute, maintain, recover, healthier, increased, significant, dramatic, local, sustainable, environmental, comparative, ecological, plastic-free, illegal, approximate, accurate, dramatic, native.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /aɪ/ in 'life,' 'biodiversity'; -ate reduction in 'climate,' 'accurate'; word stress in 'ecological,' 'plastic-free'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Asking about environmental impact, discussing conservation ideas.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Rephrasing (Individual/Pair Work):</b> Provide students with incomplete sentences or sentences to rephrase orally, focusing on the target grammar. Example prompts:
                            <ul style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Logging (affect) the forest health for many years." (Student: "Logging has significantly affected the forest health for many years.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If we (not protect) marine life, the ocean (become) less biodiverse." (Student: "If we don't protect marine life, the ocean will become less biodiverse.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Rural areas (have) more natural habitats than urban areas." (Student: "Rural areas have healthier natural habitats compared to urban areas.")</li>
                            </ul>
                            Monitor pronunciation, especially /aɪ/ and word stress. (5 minutes)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Conditionals Chain (Pair Work):</b> In pairs, students create conditional sentences (first and second) related to environmental scenarios. One student starts with a condition, and the partner completes it with a logical consequence, then offers a new condition. Example:
                            <ul style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "If we continue to produce so much plastic waste,..."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "...our rivers will accumulate more pollution. If people recycled more..."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "...we would have fewer landfills."</li>
                            </ul>
                            Encourage use of 'reduce', 'improve', 'affect'. (5 minutes)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Discussing Solutions (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs to discuss solutions for specific ecological problems presented on cards (e.g., "Problem: Declining forest health due to logging," "Problem: Low air quality in urban areas"). They must use 'present perfect' to describe the problem's current state and 'conditionals' to propose solutions, employing polite language. Example: "Deforestation has dramatically impacted our forest's biodiversity. If people planted more native trees, the forests would recover faster." (5 minutes)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Students use the language in a realistic communicative task, focusing on fluency.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: Role-play Negotiation – "Community Action for Forest Conservation"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in groups of 3-4 to role-play a community meeting or a negotiation panel discussing strategies to address the issues affecting "Our Changing Forests". Each group member will adopt a role (e.g., local resident concerned about 'biodiversity', a representative from a 'logging' company, an 'environmental' activist, or a local government official). They must negotiate environmentally responsible actions, express their concerns politely, ask follow-up questions, and propose solutions using the target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Instructions:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Assign roles and provide a brief description of each role's perspective on forest use and conservation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students should discuss the current state of local forests, citing how 'deforestation has affected' various aspects ('rivers', 'air quality', 'species').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must propose solutions using first and second conditionals (e.g., "If we limited urban expansion, would local wildlife have more space to thrive?" "If the logging company adopted more sustainable practices, we would see healthier forests.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encourage asking follow-up questions and using comparatives (e.g., "Urban ecosystems have less biodiversity compared to rural habitats.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Focus on fluent interaction, polite discourse ("Protecting our forests benefits everyone."), and collaborative problem-solving.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment and self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play, focusing on the use of target structures and vocabulary. Peer feedback could also be encouraged.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Community Action for Forest Conservation" negotiation role-play in their groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Check):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /aɪ/ and word stress in multisyllabic terms (e.g., 'dramatic', 'ecological').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Consistent and accurate use of present perfect, first and second conditionals, and comparatives.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of at least 5-7 target vocabulary words related to ecology and conservation (e.g., 'deforestation', 'biodiversity', 'sustainable', 'conservation', 'impact', 'recover').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of ideas and ability to present arguments and counter-arguments in the negotiation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective engagement in the role-play, showing ability to ask questions, respond, and contribute to the discussion.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of polite and community-focused language (e.g., "Together, we can make a difference in conservation.").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Individual):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new vocabulary or grammar structures did I use most effectively in today's discussion?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one challenge I faced when trying to express my ideas about environmental changes?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>Teacher and student reflection on learning and future steps.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (journal or quick share):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now discussing environmental issues and proposing solutions in English?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific action I or my community could take to help protect our local forests, and how would I explain it using a conditional sentence?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the speaking tasks worked best to encourage active participation and fluency? (e.g., role-play format, specific prompts).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with pronunciation or accurate use of conditionals during the speaking activities? (e.g., more targeted pronunciation drills, sentence frames for conditionals).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will transition from speaking to writing. They will use the vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced today to compose structured paragraphs or a short report about deforestation and its impact, proposing solutions, which will help consolidate their understanding and production of the target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>